`,a.jsx(e.p,{children:"This project was built as a continuation of my previous exploration into quantitative trading infrastructure and high-performance systems programming. Previously, I implemented and benchmarked different order book designs to understand how data structures, memory layouts, and algorithmic choices affect matching engine performance."}),`
`,a.jsx(e.p,{children:"While that project focused on the internal processing of orders after entering the system, this project explores the earlier stages of the trading pipeline: receiving market data, decoding packets, transporting events efficiently, and updating the order book with minimal latency."}),`
`,a.jsx(e.p,{children:"Through both projects, I learned that low-latency performance is not determined only by algorithms or Big-O complexity, but also by computer architecture and engineering decisions - cache locality, memory allocation patterns, synchronization overhead, CPU scheduling, and data movement. Concepts explored previously, including false sharing, cache line alignment, CPU affinity, lock-free programming, atomic memory ordering, zero-copy processing, memory pooling, and benchmarking-driven optimization, became central considerations when designing this pipeline."}),`
`,a.jsx(e.hr,{}),`
