`,a.jsx(e.h3,{children:"Kernel-Bypass Networking (DPDK) - Context and Inspiration"}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.p,{children:["In real trading systems, the market data ingestion layer is often one of the largest sources of latency. Traditional networking through the Linux kernel network stack introduces overhead from packet copying, system calls, interrupts, and context switching. Technologies such as the ",a.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Data Plane Development Kit (DPDK)"})," address this by letting applications bypass much of the kernel networking stack and access network interface hardware directly through user-space drivers, using techniques such as polling, huge pages, memory pools, and zero-copy packet buffers."]}),`
`,a.jsx(e.p,{children:"This project does not directly integrate DPDK, but the zero-copy parsing, memory pooling, and lock-free pipeline design were inspired by the same principles used in DPDK-based systems - the goal was to understand the software architecture behind these optimizations before extending toward hardware acceleration."}),`
