`,a.jsx(e.h2,{children:"Design Comparisons"}),`
`,a.jsx(e.p,{children:"Several comparisons were made throughout development to validate that each optimization was actually worth its complexity:"}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.ul,{children:[`
`,a.jsxs(e.li,{children:[a.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Copy vs. zero-copy parsing:"})," A traditional pipeline copies incoming network data into application buffers before decoding, adding memory movement and cache pollution. Zero-copy parsing - interpreting structures directly from existing buffers - removed this overhead and improved latency consistency."]}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.li,{children:[a.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Dynamic allocation vs. memory pool:"})," Using ",a.jsx(e.code,{children:"new"}),"/",a.jsx(e.code,{children:"delete"})," per market event introduces unpredictable latency from allocator overhead and fragmentation. The preallocated memory pool made acquisition and release constant-time operations (~1 ns), which is why production trading systems avoid frequent heap allocation on latency-critical paths."]}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.li,{children:[a.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Mutex-based queue vs. lock-free SPSC queue:"})," A mutex-based queue causes contention and scheduling overhead under high message rates. The lock-free SPSC queue, using atomics and careful memory ordering, let producer and consumer threads communicate without blocking, reaching ~80M messages/sec."]}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.li,{children:[a.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Kernel sockets vs. kernel-bypass (DPDK) networking:"})," A conventional UDP receiver relies on the Linux kernel network stack, incurring overhead from interrupts, system calls, packet copying, and context switching. DPDK-style kernel bypass removes much of this by polling network devices directly from user space. While DPDK wasn't integrated into this project, the zero-copy decoder, packet pool, and lock-free pipeline were designed around the same principles. A natural future extension would be to benchmark a standard-socket UDP receiver against a DPDK-based one to quantify the difference directly."]}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.li,{children:[a.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Software vs. hardware (FPGA) processing:"})," The FPGA prototype demonstrated deterministic, cycle-accurate latency (4ns) versus the inherent variability of software execution - illustrating why some HFT firms pair optimized software (e.g., DPDK-based pipelines) with FPGA acceleration for the most latency-sensitive components."]}),`
`]}),`
`,a.jsx(e.p,{children:"Individually, removing memcpy calls, avoiding dynamic allocation, eliminating locks, improving cache locality, and bypassing unnecessary networking layers each look like small gains - but together they compound into a significant improvement in throughput and latency consistency, mirroring the engineering philosophy of real high-frequency trading infrastructure."}),`
